    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a trio of high-efficiency starts facing favorable pitcher xERAs. **Josh Naylor** (68.45) and **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) stand as our primary point-generators; both benefit from the \"SP Skill\" boost provided by sub-5.00 xERA matchups against Nick Martinez and Cal Quantrill, respectively. While **Seiya Suzuki** (66.11) faces the toughest arm of the group in Hunter Greene, his elite park factors and top-of-the-order security make him a mandatory start. We are particularly aggressive with **Jose Caballero** (65.01) today; despite a mediocre xERA from the opposing arm, his 100% stolen base expectation against Carson Palmquist aligns perfectly with our organizational mandate to prioritize speed-heavy profiles in the daily lineup.\n\nThe benching process was rigorous, with **Austin Riley** (57.16) serving as the most difficult exclusion from the starting nine. While his raw projection is robust, he was edged out by the superior platoon-adjusted upside of **Kyle Karros** (63.20) and **Mickey Moniak** (62.51), who benefit from both wind-assisted conditions and high-leverage order positioning. We are keeping a close watch on the **James Wood** (55.03) situation; although he carries a massive 0.432 xwOBA, his matchup against a difficult southpaw and an elite hold-time pitcher creates significant risk for his stolen base potential, keeping his score in the marginal tier. \n\nManagers should note that every slot currently carries a \"Lineup Pending\" designation; final confirmation of order remains the biggest variable. We are particularly wary of the matchup between **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** against Shohei Ohtani. While both are locked into our lineup due to their positional value and skill, the sheer dominance of the opposing arm caps their daily efficiency scores, necessitating a disciplined eye on potential late-inning pinch-hit scenarios to protect our game caps.",
